  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Livestock vaccination campaign for control of epidemics in smallholder dairy animals in NW-Syria.</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="2">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">The proposed livestock vaccination campaign will protect livestock assets and livelihoods of smallholder dairy farmers and sheep- and goatherders and combat human malnutrition by ensuring the sufficient availability of milk and dairy products in NW Syria. In particular, the project seeks to rectify the dangerous insufficient vaccination coverage of dairy animals in NW Syria against major epidemic infections, focusing on Peste des Petits Ruminants (PPR) and Sheep Pox / Capripox. 
This will be achieved by addressing existing livestock vaccination gaps through large-scale refresher vaccinations of dairy sheep and goats in all accessible locations of 38 sub-districts of NW Syria.
Based on project records from different recent livestock vaccination campaigns conducted by FAO and partner NGOs in 2021 in addition to a current survey conducted by the partner NGOs in April 2022, the target numbers are up to 1,475,000 sheep and goats owned by 40,000 sheepherder households (240,000 beneficiaries ). 
Based on data from the previous vaccination campaigns conducted in 2021, the target number of women-headed beneficiary households was 15% to 17%. About 30% of sheepherders are expected to be IDP households. 
From the analysis of FSL Cluster Livestock Technical Working Group (LTWG) veterinary vaccination mapping data and taking into account LTWG recommendations and the seasonality of livestock vaccinations in Syria, the following epidemics have been prioritized: (1) Peste des Petits Ruminants (PPR) for sheep and goats, (2) Sheep Pox (also called Capripox or Goat Pox) for sheep and goats. The target is to cover at least 80% of sheep amp goats with vaccinations against PPR and Capripox.</narrative>
  </description>
